1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,280
He became the Luftwaffe's most successful fighter pilot, and only two hours later he vanished over the English Channel.

2
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:08,200
His name was Helmut Wick.

3
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:13,980
He was born in Mannheim in 1915 and joined the Luftwaffe in 1936.

4
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:19,420
In training he was seen as an average pilot, and he passed one course only at the second attempt.

5
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:25,640
In Fighter Wing 53 he learned to fly the Messerschmitt Bf 109 under Werner Mölders.

6
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:28,700
When the war began he joined Fighter Wing 2 Richthofen.

7
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:35,820
In November 1939 he shot down a French fighter near Nancy, his first victory.

8
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:40,420
In the Battle of France and the Battle of Britain his score rose quickly.

9
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:46,540
In October 1940 he became the fourth soldier of the Wehrmacht to receive the Oak Leaves to the Knight's Cross.

10
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:52,100
Shortly afterwards, at 25, he became commander of the wing, the youngest in the Luftwaffe.

11
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:55,480
Propaganda presented him to the foreign press as a hero.

12
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:05,080
On 28 November 1940 he flew with his staff flight to the Isle of Wight and shot down a Spitfire, his 55th victory.

13
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:06,920
That put him ahead of Mölders.

14
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:10,780
After landing in Cherbourg he had his aircraft refuelled and took off again.

15
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:13,740
Over the island he shot down another Spitfire.

16
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:18,480
Shortly afterwards, Spitfires of the British 609 Squadron attacked his flight.

17
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:23,020
Wick's aircraft was hit, probably by the British pilot John Dundas.

18
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:29,120
His wingman Rudolf Pflanz shot down the Spitfire at once, and Dundas went into the sea with it.

19
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:35,240
Pflanz had seen the German pilot bail out with his parachute, and later reported a dinghy.

20
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:40,500
Torpedo boats searched all night, and the next day aircraft and the sea rescue service.

21
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:42,160
Wick was never found.

22
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:48,060
He was credited with 56 victories, and it was his 168th combat mission.

23
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:50,680
Helmut Wick was 25 years old.
